<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karakorum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karakorum was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, established under Ögedei. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> was subordinate to the Great Khan in Karakorum during Chagatai's lifetime.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karakorum.jpg" alt="Karakorum" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erdene_Zuu_Monastery_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Orkhon valley had been a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> empires. The Göktürks placed the seat of power, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otuken">Ötüken</a>, in the nearby Khangai Mountains, and the Uyghur capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Karabalgasun</a> lay 27 kilometres north-west of where Karakorum later stood. The site suited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>: it was central to the growing empire, the climate in the late Medieval Warm Period gave abundant grass, the Orkhon ran north to south where most rivers run east to west, and its symbolic importance as a former seat of power expressed Mongol control over the region's largely non-Mongol population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1218–1219 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> gathered his troops at a place called Karakorum for the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, and the foundation of a city is usually dated to 1220. Until 1235 it seems to have been little more than a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yurt">yurt</a> town. After defeating the Jin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a> had walls and a fixed palace built; the Tumen Amgalan Ord, the Palace of Myriad Peace, was decreed in 1235 and finished within a year. Ögedei encouraged the empire's elite to build houses near it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> enlarged the palace and had the great stupa temple completed. An enslaved Parisian goldsmith, Guillaume Boucher, made the Silver Tree for the central courtyard, with silver fruit, four golden serpents and a trumpet-sounding angel, which dispensed drinks to guests.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a>, a Flemish Franciscan envoy, reached the city in 1254. He thought the royal abbey at Saint-Denis ten times grander than the palace, yet described a cosmopolitan and religiously tolerant town with four gates, quarters for the Saracenes and for the Cathai, twelve temples, two mosques and a Nestorian church. After the empire divided in 1260, Khubilai Khan favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, and Karakorum became a provincial administrative centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> took it in 1277 and was driven out the next year, and it prospered again in the first half of the 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the Yuan fell in 1368 Karakorum housed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayushiridara">Biligtü Khan</a> from 1370. Ming armies occupied it in 1372 and in 1380 took and razed it. It was inhabited at the start of the 16th century, when Batu-Möngke Dayan Khan made it a capital again, but after changing hands between Oirads and Chinggisids it was finally abandoned. In 1585 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abtai-sain-khan">Abtai Sain Khan</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu Monastery</a> from its stones and bricks. Nikolai Yadrintsev conclusively identified the site in 1889, and the German-Mongolian excavations of 2000–2004 suggest that the building Soviet and Mongolian excavators took for Ögedei's palace was the great stupa temple. The ruins lie in the Orkhon Valley World Heritage Site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karakorum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Smbat's embassy to <b>Karakorum</b> brought back in 1250 a guarantee of Cilicia's integrity; Het'um I visited Möngke Khan in 1253 and fought under Hulagu at Aleppo and Damascus in 1259–1260, where Arab historians charge his troops with a massacre and arson in the main mosque.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During Chagatai's lifetime the khanate was plainly subordinate to the Great Khan in <b>Karakorum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1235 an assembly (quriltai) at <b>Karakorum</b> ordered a great campaign to the west, and from 1236 Batu, with the general Subutai and princes of all branches of the ruling house, conquered Volga Bulgaria, the Kipchak (Cuman) steppe and the principalities of Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The new capital at <b>Karakorum</b>, founded under Ögedei in the 1230s, drew merchants and craftsmen from across Eurasia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1243 the Mongols decided who held the grand-princely title, by issuing a patent, the yarlyk, to the prince they favoured, and claimants travelled to the Horde at Sarai, and sometimes to <b>Karakorum</b>, to receive it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol rulers exempted the clergy of many religions from taxation, and in 1254 the Franciscan William of Rubruck took part in a debate between Christians, Muslims and Buddhists at the court of Möngke in <b>Karakorum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
